   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network carries a video stream from the internet to a laptop through a series of steps involving the router, access points, radio channels, and packets. When you start streaming a video on your laptop, the request is sent to the internet, and the video data is broken down into small packets. These packets travel through the internet to your home network.\n\nYour home router acts as the central hub, connecting your devices to the internet. It receives the video packets and forwards them to the access point, which is often the same device as the router or a separate Wi-Fi extender. The access point then broadcasts the video packets through the air using radio waves on a specific channel. Your laptop, equipped with a Wi-Fi receiver, picks up these radio waves and decodes the packets to reconstruct the video stream.\n\nThe radio channels used by Wi-Fi networks are like lanes on a highway. Each device on your network, including your laptop, uses a specific lane to transmit and receive data. When two devices try to transmit at the same time, it can cause a collision, similar to a traffic jam. To avoid this, Wi-Fi networks use a protocol called CSMA/CA (Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Avoidance). This protocol allows devices to listen to the channel"
